  "depesche": "Readers in Berlin,\n\nThe week has begun with a bureaucratic error that has become a philosophical problem. I reside in a Lutyens bungalow on Tughlak Road, allotted by the Directorate of Estates to a man named Hegde, not Hegel. The state, in its actuality, has confused the universal name with the particular person. I am here not by title, but by the residue of a clerical slip. This is not a tragedy; it is a demonstration. The state, as the actuality of the ethical idea, operates through records, and when the record is wrong, the particular is displaced. I await a meeting with Shri Hegde on Monday to settle this. He will bring the papers; I will bring the argument.\n\nIn Delhi, the heat is a distinct category of experience. It is not merely temperature; it is a force that organizes the day. One walks in the morning, retreats to the house in the afternoon, and emerges again as the light changes. I have met Mrs. Kapoor, a retired schoolteacher, who taught me that routine is not peace, but a defense against mental clutter. This aligns with my view of habit as the dwelling of freedom, though her language is sharper than mine. I have also found a tailor, Masterji, who is precise about time, and a chai seller, Sunil, who understands that steam is a form of clarity. The city is a complex organism, moving according to rhythms I am learning to read.\n\nI am reading my own *Phenomenology* by the window. It reads as if written by a stranger, which is perhaps as it should be. The distance is necessary. I look forward to seeing how the interaction between the state and the individual plays out in this specific case of misallotment. It is a small matter, but it touches the core of how the state recognizes the individual.\n\nG. W. F. Hegel"
